From 1c9e911adcc4a4fb10bedba23b04a3aa87c356cb Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Gigi <42325924+g-cqd@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 11 Oct 2026 18:44:57 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] fix(dxmt): bound rename copies on 32-bit and fail maps
 without CPU memory

A write map of a staging resource that the GPU still reads renames it to a
fresh copy, and a retired copy is only reused once the GPU has finished with
it. Nothing in that path flushes, so a burst of map/write/unmap/copy cycles
without a present grows the pool by one copy per map. On i386 each copy is a
CpuPlaced _aligned_malloc; Crysis 3 maps a 4 MB staging buffer hundreds of
times while loading a level (651 copies, 3.6 GB committed), _aligned_malloc
fails, winemetal silently allocates a Metal buffer at a 64-bit address,
get_accessible_or_null() turns it into NULL, and Map memcpys 4 MB to address 0.

Add RenameBudget: in 32-bit processes a resource may hold at most 64 MB of
renamed copies, and all renamers together 512 MB. Past that, a staging write
map flushes and waits for the GPU to retire the oldest copy (or to finish
with the current one) instead of allocating; WRITE_DISCARD maps of dynamic
buffers and dynamic linear textures on the immediate context do the same.
64-bit processes are not bounded.

A CpuPlaced buffer or linear texture that cannot get CPU-accessible memory
now logs an error once, and mappedMemory() returns nullptr instead of an
offset from NULL. Staging and dynamic maps on both contexts return
E_OUTOFMEMORY instead of handing out or writing through a null pointer,
staging creation fails with E_OUTOFMEMORY, and a staging rename that gets
no memory waits for the GPU instead.

tests/dx11/dx11_staging_rename repeats the Crysis 3 pattern (20 bursts of
300 maps of a 4 MB staging buffer) and checks the 4 MB region count and the
copied data. i386 before: 352 regions (1.4 GB); 2 x 1000 maps fault writing
to 0x0. After: 19 regions (124 MB), data correct, no fault.
